    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "1. Can baby teeth cause bite problems later? Yes. The child’s baby teeth guide where the permanent teeth erupt. Early tooth loss, crowding, or habits can shift developing teeth. So, a minor issue in baby teeth can grow into a larger alignment problem later. 2. Does mouth breathing affect teeth and gums? Yes. Mouth breathing dries oral tissues and can irritate gums. Also, it often links with poor tongue posture and narrow arches. Over time, that can support crowding and bite changes, plus higher gum inflammation risk. 3. When do braces work better than aligners? Braces often work best for severe crowding, rotations, and complex bite shifts. They give strong control for moving teeth and closing gaps. Aligners fit mild to moderate cases. Your exam decides what fits your bite. 4. Do severe cases need jaw surgery? Some severe skeletal cases need jaw surgery or orthognathic surgery because tooth movement alone cannot fix jaw imbalance. Surgery repositions jaw bones, then braces or aligners fine-tune the bite. Your orthodontist and surgeon plan it together. 5. How long does malocclusion treatment take? Time depends on the bite issue, crowding level, growth stage, and your plan. Many cases run from months to a couple of years. Retainers follow to hold results. Strong home care helps prevent delays and gum issues."
    }
